#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d72837a-6c83-5f35-929e-ffa5344f19be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63.43}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305610} {:answer/id #uuid "f974f885-0f17-5500-a7fe-b3bd99079839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305611} {:answer/id #uuid "c52c118f-55a2-5a15-92a7-015727f1d9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77.07}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305612} {:answer/id #uuid "38a10d4d-48eb-58bc-ab1d-69d971c1060c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26.57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305613} {:answer/id #uuid "be57eafa-5a3e-5467-8488-646d3a30fbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a10d4d-48eb-58bc-ab1d-69d971c1060c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26.57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305613} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f81a4f-4f23-590d-a87a-a991a42d8c5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e36ca8d6-b65d-5cd3-83a7-070f0b3504a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-77167" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(2t,4,3),$ $B(5t-1,4,3)$ and $C(2t+4,t+5,3).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=8.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩,\\,b=⟨5t-1,\\,4,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+4,\\,t+5,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨5t-1,\\,4,\\,3⟩-⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+4,\\,t+5,\\,3⟩-⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,t+1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=8,$ we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =8 \\\\ (3t-1)\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (t+1)+0\\cdot 0 & =8 \\\\ 4(3t-1)+0+0 & =8 \\\\ 12t-4 & =8 \\\\ 12t & =12 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{80}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})≈{26.57}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
